+## Caller credentials for NSS modules
+
+Because `nsncd` performs every lookup in its own process, an NSS module that
+identifies its caller (for example through `SO_PEERCRED` on a socket it opens,
+or `getuid()`) sees `nsncd` rather than the process that asked. While a request
+is being handled, the worker thread has the client's `SO_PEERCRED` (pid, uid
+and gid of the process that connected to `/var/run/nscd/socket`) available to
+modules through an exported C function:
+
+```c
+#include <dlfcn.h>
+#include <sys/socket.h>
+
+int (*nsncd_peer_cred)(struct ucred *) = dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT, "nsncd_peer_cred");
+struct ucred caller;
+if (nsncd_peer_cred != NULL && nsncd_peer_cred(&caller) == 0) {
+    /* caller.pid, caller.uid, caller.gid */
+}
+```
+
+`dlsym` returns null when the module is not running inside `nsncd` (or inside
+an `nsncd` without this feature); the function returns -1 when the calling
+thread is not handling a client request. A module that acts on these values
+trusts `nsncd` to relay them honestly; the values themselves come from the
+kernel.
+
 ## Bug Reports and Contributions
 
 Please create GitHub issues and/or pull requests.
